pci: pcie_dw_rockchip: mirror vendor U-Boot reset and link-up delays

The Rockchip vendor U-Boot (radxa/u-boot next-dev-v2024.10, as shipped by
Armbian for the Orange Pi 5 until late 2025) brings PCIe up with far more
generous delays than mainline:

  - 200 ms with PERST# asserted (mainline: 100 ms)
  - 20 ms after releasing PERST#, before polling the link (mainline: none)
  - 1000 ms after the link reports up, before the first Configuration
    Request (mainline: none)

Mainline issues the first Configuration Request the moment the link is up.
PCIe r6.0 sec 6.6.1 requires at least 100 ms there. Some NVMe drives train
their link quickly but are not yet ready to answer, so on a cold boot the
root bridge enumerates with no endpoint, bootstd finds no NVMe bootdev and
U-Boot falls back to PXE (siderolabs/sbc-rockchip#54). A warm reset leaves
the drive already initialised, which is why "reset" at the prompt sometimes
worked.

Mirror the vendor timings. Costs about 1.1 s of boot time per PCIe port.

See also: https://www.mail-archive.com/u-boot@lists.u-boot-project.org/msg02753.html
          (spec-minimum variant of the same fix, siderolabs/sbc-rockchip#122)

--- a/drivers/pci/pcie_dw_rockchip.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/pcie_dw_rockchip.c
@@ -263,10 +263,16 @@
 	 * Express Card Electromechanical Specification, 1.1. However, we don't
 	 * know if the refclk is coming from RC's PHY or external OSC. If it's
 	 * from RC, so enabling LTSSM is the just right place to release #PERST.
+	 *
+	 * The Rockchip vendor U-Boot (used by Armbian) keeps PERST# asserted
+	 * for 200 ms instead of the 100 ms minimum and then waits a further
+	 * 20 ms after releasing it before polling the link. Mirror that: some
+	 * NVMe drives (e.g. on Orange Pi 5) are otherwise missed on cold boot.
 	 */
-	mdelay(100);
+	mdelay(200);
 	if (dm_gpio_is_valid(&priv->rst_gpio))
 		dm_gpio_set_value(&priv->rst_gpio, 1);
+	mdelay(20);
 
 	/* Check if the link is up or not */
 	for (retries = 0; retries < 10; retries++) {
@@ -285,6 +291,15 @@
 	dev_info(priv->dw.dev, "PCIe Link up, LTSSM is 0x%x\n",
 		 rk_pcie_readl_apb(priv, PCIE_CLIENT_LTSSM_STATUS));
 	rk_pcie_debug_dump(priv);
+
+	/*
+	 * PCIe r6.0 sec 6.6.1 requires at least 100 ms between the end of
+	 * Link Training and the first Configuration Request for ports faster
+	 * than 5.0 GT/s. The vendor U-Boot waits a full 1000 ms here ("link
+	 * maybe in Gen switch recovery"); do the same so that a fast-training
+	 * endpoint is ready to answer configuration requests.
+	 */
+	mdelay(1000);
 	return 0;
 }
 
